    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d217">

                <pb facs="0951" n="920" sameAs="frus:frus1981-88v05/pg920"/>

                <head>217. Note From the Assistant Secretary of State for European and Canadian
                    Affairs (Ridgway) to Secretary of State
                        Shultz<anchor n="1" xml:id="d217-f1"
                    /></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>Washington</placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1986-04-21">April 21, 1986</date></dateline>
                    <salute>Mr. Secretary:</salute>
                </opener>
                <p>Mark Palmer and Mark Parris had one of their informal sessions
                    with the Soviet Embassy’s Sokolov Friday
                    evening. Their memcon is attached.<anchor n="2" xml:id="d217-f2"/></p>
                <p>Sokolov’s claim that the Soviets tried to
                    be helpful before the Berlin bombing and are trying now to prevent further
                    incidents, while self-serving, bears following up.<anchor n="3" xml:id="d217-f3"
                    /> While I think it best to hold this information very closely, we will be in
                    touch with Bob Oakley to see if we can
                    pass data which would put the Soviets to the test.</p>
                <p>Sokolov’s message that rescheduling the
                        Shevardnadze visit will have to await
                    the arrival of a new Soviet Ambassador confirms our sense that a summer summit
                    is no longer in the cards. If we are to get the process back on track at all,
                    however, we ought to pay attention to his entreaty to keep scheduled meetings on
                    track. In the first instance, this means meeting our commitment to send
                        Elliot Abrams to Moscow <pb
                        facs="0952" n="921" xml:id="pg921"/> in mid-May, despite ARA’s preference to
                    cancel or send a lower-ranking representative.<anchor n="4" xml:id="d217-f4"/>
                    You have a separate decision memo on that issue.</p>
                <p>I’ve also attached a report from the British Embassy in Moscow which suggests
                    that the decision to cancel the Shevardnadze visit was not an easy one, and that Gorbachev’s discomfort with Qadhafi is acute.<anchor n="5" xml:id="d217-f5"
                    /> This tracks with Sokolov’s own sense
                    that Moscow is anxious to limit damage to the U.S.-Soviet relationship.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed><hi rendition="#bold">Rozanne L.
                                Ridgway</hi><anchor n="6" xml:id="d217-f6"/></signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d217-f1" n="1" type="source" xml:id="d217-f1-content">
                <p>Source: Reagan Library, George Shultz
                    Papers, 4D, 1986 Soviet Union April. Secret; Sensitive.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d217-f2" n="2" xml:id="d217-f2-content">
                <p>On April 18, Palmer and Parris met with Sokolov in the bar at the Watergate Hotel from 6 until 8 p.m.
                    The memorandum of conversation is attached but not printed.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d217-f3" n="3" xml:id="d217-f3-content">
                <p>Reference is to the April 5 bombing of the La Belle disco in West Berlin. See
                        <ref target="#d216">Document 216</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d217-f4" n="4" xml:id="d217-f4-content">
                <p>Abrams was tentatively scheduled to attend
                    U.S.-Soviet talks in Moscow on Central America and the Caribbean.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d217-f5" n="5" xml:id="d217-f5-content">
                <p>Attached but not printed.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d217-f6" n="6" xml:id="d217-f6-content">
                <p>Ridgway signed “RR” above her typed signature.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
